GROUP ANAGRAMS:

Brute Force — Compare Every String

One simple idea:

Take a string.
Compare it with every other string.
Sort both strings.
If sorted versions are equal → they are anagrams.
Keep track of strings already grouped.

For example:

eat → aet
tea → aet
tan → ant
ate → aet
nat → ant
bat → abt

class Solution {
    public List<List<String>> groupAnagrams(String[] strs) {

        List<List<String>> result = new ArrayList<>();
        boolean[] used = new boolean[strs.length];

        for (int i = 0; i < strs.length; i++) {

            if (used[i])
                continue;

            List<String> group = new ArrayList<>();
            group.add(strs[i]);
            used[i] = true;

            char[] first = strs[i].toCharArray();
            Arrays.sort(first);

            for (int j = i + 1; j < strs.length; j++) {

                if (used[j])
                    continue;

                char[] current = strs[j].toCharArray();
                Arrays.sort(current);

                if (Arrays.equals(first, current)) {
                    group.add(strs[j]);
                    used[j] = true;
                }
            }

            result.add(group);
        }

        return result;
    }
}

Optimized — HashMap + Sorted String Key

Instead of comparing every string with every other string, create a key for each string.

Example:

eat → aet
tea → aet
ate → aet

tan → ant
nat → ant

bat → abt

Then use:

HashMap<key, list of strings>

So:

aet → [eat, tea, ate]
ant → [tan, nat]
